<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Overview</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice is a long-term gaming project I founded to support collaborative tabletop campaigns,
    persistent game servers, and the technical systems behind them. What began as a Dungeons &amp; Dragons group
    developed into a connected project involving campaign operations, participant-centered experience design,
    extensive documentation, community coordination, self-hosted infrastructure, and purpose-built web tooling.
</p>
<p>
    The professional value of the project comes from its scale and continuity. I was not only preparing individual
    game sessions. I was designing and operating an adaptable environment in which participants could make meaningful
    choices, pursue unexpected goals, and remain connected across changing schedules, locations, and technical needs.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Project History And Scope</h2>
<p>
    I founded Dorks &amp; Dice on September 6, 2024. Fool's Gold became the group's principal long-running campaign
    and concluded in late April 2026, shortly before my graduation. During summer 2025, I ran an Acquisitions Incorporated
    campaign so members remaining for the summer semester could continue playing without advancing Fool's Gold while
    other players were away.
</p>
<p>
    The project later expanded beyond scheduled tabletop sessions. I launched a Minecraft server on October 17, 2025,
    followed by a Hytale server on January 15, 2026. Those services, the group's Discord presence, and the Dorks &amp; Dice
    website extended the project into community coordination, Linux server administration, networking, deployment,
    troubleshooting, and ongoing service ownership.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Scale Of Campaign Operations</h2>
<p>
    Fool's Gold sessions lasted approximately 12 hours, including lunch and dinner breaks. I typically invested at least
    two dozen additional hours each week preparing scenarios, encounters, characters, locations, rules interactions,
    continuity details, and contingencies.
</p>
<p>
    That preparation was intentionally broader than a fixed story outline. I did not want my own lack of preparation to
    become an artificial limit on what the players could attempt. For almost anything they wanted to do, my goal was to
    work with them to determine how it could happen, what resources or risks it involved, and how the world would respond.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Prepared multiple viable paths rather than requiring predetermined solutions</li>
    <li>Adapted plans in real time while preserving long-term continuity and meaningful consequences</li>
    <li>Facilitated extended live sessions requiring sustained communication, judgment, and rapid decision-making</li>
    <li>Managed scheduling, expectations, and significant interpersonal conflict while keeping the project moving</li>
    <li>Maintained detailed records across interconnected characters, locations, factions, mechanics, and unresolved events</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Research-Driven, Player-Centered Design</h2>
<p>
    I expanded the setting in response to the interests and capabilities of the players rather than treating its original
    scope as a permanent boundary. When one player built a paladin around blacksmithing and item invention, I studied basic
    blacksmithing and metallurgy so materials, repairs, crafting decisions, and custom equipment could have greater depth
    and internal consistency.
</p>
<p>
    I applied the same approach to the wider rules ecosystem. Because I maintain a zero-tolerance policy toward piracy,
    I collected lawful access to the complete fifth-edition book library so unexpected directions would not be limited by
    unavailable source material. Naval play could lead to vehicle design; vehicle design could lead to flight; and sufficiently
    developed flight and magic could eventually lead to interstellar conflict. I prepared across those systems so player
    ambition could expand the campaign rather than be rejected because the relevant material was unavailable.
</p>
<p>
    This required learning unfamiliar domains, identifying the intent behind a participant's request, and integrating new
    systems into an existing world without breaking its established rules or continuity. The result was a campaign that
    became deeper through interaction instead of merely becoming larger through added content.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Case Study: The Fool's Gold Finale</h2>
<p>
    The final conflict demonstrates how those systems converged. The campaign did not begin with plans for interstellar
    travel, but by the time the players had developed the means to pursue it, I was prepared to support it using the world's
    existing history and metaphysics.
</p>
<p>
    The party opened a portal roughly the size of a city into another solar system. The portal consumed approximately
    one quarter of the stored power previously extracted from a star, with stars functioning as gods within the setting.
    That earlier extraction had been performed by the Forclaimers, whose current emperor, Godric III, became the campaign's
    final opponent.
</p>
<p>
    The finale was structured as a multi-hour, multi-theater operation rather than a conventional single-target encounter:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Half of the party fought Godric III directly</li>
    <li>The remaining party members piloted their mech dragon against Godric's mech dragon in the sky above the primary fight</li>
    <li>Spelljammer mechanics supported operation of the player-controlled mech dragon</li>
    <li>Allied forces broke into the sun extractor to destroy it</li>
    <li>The army of the country founded by the player characters fought Godric's forces with assistance from rebel allies</li>
    <li>Xanu, an artificial god of souls allied with the party, controlled soul-powered mechanical dinosaurs</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The encounter remained challenging for hours even though the final blow against Godric III dealt 1,372 damage. That
    outcome was possible because the fight did not depend on a single standard health bar. It depended on parallel objectives,
    accumulated alliances, specialized equipment, encounter mechanics, and consequences developed across the campaign.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Encounter Systems And Software Direction</h2>
<p>
    Loot Tavern's crafting systems gave players meaningful control over their equipment, but the resulting power scaling
    made conventional single-target bosses easy to eliminate before they could meaningfully participate. Rather than remove
    player choices or invalidate the equipment they had created, I adapted the kaiju encounter mechanics from
    <em>Ryoko's Guide to the Yokai Realms</em> for bosses that were not physically kaiju-sized.
</p>
<p>
    These encounters can involve separate weak-point health pools, rage, active mechanics, evolving states, and standard
    initiative at the same time. They produced more interesting and durable boss fights, but they also created a significant
    tracking burden during live play.
</p>
<p>
    My first planned Dorks &amp; Dice web tool is a personal-use initiative and encounter tracker designed around that problem.
    The initial version will manage standard initiative alongside weak points, rage, encounter mechanics, and boss state.
    It is intended to turn a proven manual workflow into a clearer and more reliable software-supported process.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Technical Operations</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice also became a practical infrastructure project. The Minecraft and Hytale servers required hardware
    planning, Linux and container administration, networking, remote access, service configuration, deployment,
    troubleshooting, and long-term maintenance. The website provides a durable public home for the group and a future
    delivery platform for the DM tools developed from these campaign requirements.
</p>
<p>
    This work connects software development with real operational ownership. The systems are not isolated demonstrations;
    they exist to support an actual group, persistent services, and workflows developed through sustained use.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">What This Shows About Me</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice is one of the clearest examples of how I approach complex projects. I begin with the people using the
    system, learn what they are trying to accomplish, research unfamiliar domains when necessary, and build flexible
    structures that support meaningful choices without abandoning consistency or maintainability.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Long-term project ownership and execution</li>
    <li>Participant-centered requirements discovery and experience design</li>
    <li>Systems thinking across creative, organizational, and technical domains</li>
    <li>Leadership, facilitation, expectation-setting, and conflict resolution</li>
    <li>Research and rapid domain learning</li>
    <li>Complex state, dependency, and continuity management</li>
    <li>Linux server, networking, web development, and deployment experience</li>
    <li>Identification of real workflow problems that can be converted into software products</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Current Status</h2>
<p>
    Fool's Gold and the summer 2025 Acquisitions Incorporated campaign are complete. The Minecraft and Hytale servers,
    Dorks &amp; Dice website, and broader project identity preserve the foundation for future activity. My immediate software
    direction is the initiative and complex encounter tracker, followed by additional Game Master tools based on workflows
    I experienced directly while running the campaigns.
</p>

